WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.900
With a full roster of spooky ghosts and a
literal room full of references, Annabelle

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.090
Comes Home is stuffed with small details as
opposed to the original Annabelle doll, which

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.360
is only stuffed with cotton.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.720
Or whatever old Raggedy Anne dolls were stuffed
with.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.080
Fiberglass?

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:18.300
Maybe that's why everyone's scared to touch
it it's devilishly itchy.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.800
I'm not gonna touch the doll.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:20.900
I'm not gonna touch it."

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.440
Call the babysitter, because we're diving
deep into all the small details in Annabelle

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.520
Comes Home, and we're conjuring up plenty
of spoilers along the way.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:33.640
Right at the beginning, Annabelle Comes Home
puts viewers on familiar ground.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.120
If you didn't notice, the story picks up right
where we first met Annabelle all those years

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.440
ago, in 2013's The Conjuring.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.800
That film starts with the porcelain poltergeist
terrorizing some girls in an apartment before

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.280
the Warrens step in to take that mean old
doll off their hands.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.420
Annabelle Come Home opens on a continuation
of that scene, which both grounds the story

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:58.120
for longtime fans and gives it a firm chronological
place in the Conjuring universe.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.739
Of course, we also get a little insight into
why the Warrens are so scared of Annabelle,

00:01:02.739 --> 00:01:07.259
since the doll decides to bring half a cemetery
back to life to terrorize the couple on the

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:08.020
drive home.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.460
Geez, Annabelle, can't you chill for even
one night?

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:14.980
"Honey, YOKAY?"

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:22.740
The one place you never want to go in the
Warrens' house is into the artifact room.

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:27.659
That door has eighteen locks on it for a reason
there's nothing on the other side but unimaginable

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:28.300
horror.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:31.600
"Prepare to enter the scary door."

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.640
At least, that's true in the film world.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:39.800
For viewers, the artifact room is undoubtedly
a treasure trove of Easter eggs and references.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:44.320
There, we can see the music box from the first
Conjuring, right where Ed left it at the end

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:44.820
of the film.

00:01:45.321 --> 00:01:48.560
There are also items that haven't shown up
in the Conjuring world, but are taken from

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:49.880
the real world instead.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:55.340
The book The Black Shuck alludes to a mythical
ghost dog that supposedly roams the English

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:55.940
countryside.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.460
And the Ferryman is a figure in multiple mythologies.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:04.299
In Greek mythology, he was a dude named Charon
who ferried souls across the River Styx for

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:04.800
a fee.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:06.300
You like dead people, right?

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.520
I got a few dead people you might recognize.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:10.119
Check this out.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:14.780
Jackson, Grant, and look who's joining the
party: Benjamin Franklin."

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:20.120
Like a teenage girl with a crush, or a mailman,
Annabelle is a big fan of leaving notes.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.900
"You always leave a note"

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:27.820
Specifically, she leaves notes with the spooky
question "Miss me?" when she's in the throes

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.800
of haunting the living daylights out of someone.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.500
This little detail goes all the way back to
the first Conjuring film.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:38.320
In that intro sequence, the girls find the
message scrawled in red crayon on a crumpled

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:39.140
piece of paper.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:43.800
In Annabelle Comes Home, the motif shows up
in several different places, notably on the

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.420
haunted typewriter in the Warrens' artifact
room.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:51.620
Also worth mentioning is that the repetition,
and the use of the typewriter, is reminiscent

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:56.790
of Jack Torrance's "All work and no play makes
Jack a dull boy" typewriter breakdown in The

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:57.280
Shining.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.700
That may or may not have been intentional,
but it's a nice nod to a horror classic if

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:02.380
it was.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:07.580
One of the Easter eggs in Annabelle Comes
Home happens early in the movie, when Mary

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.140
Ellen is in the supermarket.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:14.020
She runs into her friend Daniela, and the
two stop in front of a rack of comic books.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.160
They were only onscreen for a second, but
the specific comics really say a lot.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.460
First of all, they're all DC comics.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:25.000
No big surprise there the Annabelle films
are owned by Warner Bros., which also owns

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.440
the DC extended universe.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.020
You can catch the cover of a Detective Comics
issue, as well as an issue of Strange Adventures,

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:35.540
starring the world's most super-powered archaeologist,
Adam Strange.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:41.380
"Beware the jub jub bird and shun the froooomiest
bandersnatch.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.000
Got your nose!

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:43.500
Heh heh heh!"

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:47.860
But most fittingly, Annabelle Comes Home director
Gary Dauberman revealed that there's also

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.720
a Swamp Thing comic on the rack.

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:55.520
For those who don't know, Dauberman also co-created
the DC series Swamp Thing, which was sadly

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:58.500
canceled before the entire first season could
even finish airing.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.800
Ed Warren was a man of many talents he was
an author, a public speaker, and a ghost chaser,

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.520
and on top of it all he was really good at
convincing people to pay him for all these

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:09.020
things.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:13.780
But fewer people know that he was also essentially
the Bob Ross of haunted houses.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:17.140
"That building, that building looks as old
as me.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:18.520
And that's oooold."

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:21.360
Eddy's paintings have shown up in Conjuring
films before.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.260
At least, fictionalized versions have.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:27.979
The one most fans know about is the painting
of the demon nun in The Conjuring 2.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:32.460
But Annabelle Comes Home introduces a new
painting in Ed Warren's signature style and

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:36.800
not just any painting, but what looks like
a rendering of the house featured in the previous

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.180
film, Annabelle: Creation.

00:04:38.729 --> 00:04:42.660
It's not a perfect re-creation, but the placement
of the tree and the remote house are pretty

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:43.780
much unmistakable.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:45.200
Spooooky!

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:50.470
For those who don't know, the so-called real-life
Annabelle doll doesn't look anything like

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:52.820
the eerie porcelain doll we see in the films.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.160
In fact, the real thing looks downright goofy
it's just a Raggedy Ann doll.

00:05:01.289 --> 00:05:05.599
The story behind it is essentially the same,
although no doubt embellished for the silver

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:06.100
screen.

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:10.620
Some girl got the doll, weird stuff started
happening, and the Warrens eventually stepped

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:11.600
in to save the day.

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:15.880
Annabelle Comes Home did pay a little bit
of fan service to the real doll in the form

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:18.520
of a quick clip of a gameshow on the TV.

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:22.680
Don't blink, and you'll see a contestant winning
a classic Raggedy Ann.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.360
It's not unusual for the people behind the
scenes of a film to slip their names into

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.820
some text onscreen.

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.800
Annabelle Comes Home is no different.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.080
During the scene at Judy's school, Judy inexplicably
stops to stare at the bust of the school's

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:38.580
namesake, Father Michael Thomas Morrissey.

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:43.060
The name doesn't have any connection to the
Conjuring universe or any of the Warrens'

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:43.560
investigations.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.800
But it does bear more than a passing resemblance
to Annabelle Comes Home executive producer

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.460
Michelle Morrissey.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:50.700
Coincidence?

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:55.280
In the Conjuring universe, there are no coincidences
only spin-offs.

